WEBVTT

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.960
Most applications connect to more than
one backend, like one for development, one

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.360
for your testers, and
one for your customers.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.100
Creating and maintaining a separate build
of the app for each backend takes extra

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:26.980
time and effort. With Profiles,
you create one build, and

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.080
that build contains all
of your environments.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.860
A profile is a

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:41.500
small JSON file that contains key value
pairs, such as your API host or your sign-in settings.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.570
To add a profile
in Studio, open the Resources

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.690
panel and click Import Resource.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:54.790
Go to the Resources folder inside Web App
and create a new folder called Profiles.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.570
Open the Profiles folder, click Upload
Files and choose the JSON file for each

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:05.430
environment. Use this folder and not
the Profiles folder at the top of the project

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.790
because that folder holds
the back-end properties files.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.380
Next, in your app configuration, you list
the profiles to include in this build, and

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.660
you choose the profile
that the app opens with.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.400
Profiles that are not in
this list are not included.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.500
The file can remain in your
project, but the app cannot use it.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.440
You then build the app one time.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:40.660
Inside the app, all of these values are
available in one object, called global.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.360
In a widget, you bind a property to a key,
in the same way that you bind it to any

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.160
other value. In a script, you
read the value from app.global.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:59.200
In both cases, the value always comes
from the profile that is currently active.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.450
For example, a tester might want to
connect the app to the staging environment.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.580
You get the list of profiles from the app,
show them in a drop-down, and when the

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.880
user selects one, you call set profile.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.580
When that call is complete, you call
refresh, and the screen shows the new values.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.840
The app also saves the
selected profile on the device.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.480
When the user closes the app and opens
it again, the app starts with the same profile

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.080
These values are packaged inside the app.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.520
So anyone who has the app can read them.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.720
Only store values such as URLs,
client IDs and feature flags.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.820
Do not store passwords or
secrets in these files.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.540
Multi profile environments let you
use one build for every environment.
